e snapshots, unchanged configuration hashes, and recovered services, time synchronization, and SSH.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6dd6c22125474c40","cmd-272912a69de9443b","cmd-1e66698c845f4a94","cmd-d1af6e1ede9a4311","cmd-606e46b5df3442c1","cmd-b4fd34fa0ab04e4a","cmd-ca720af005b542d2","cmd-95f956f3557b4099","cmd-888ccdc37a35421c","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied. Node1 suppresses service-account core payloads while retaining crash-event logging, and the behavior remained effective after a directly observed reboot. The independent node-restart protocol is complete and coherent.","limitations":["Behavioral crash testing covered the chrony service account (UID 995), not every possible local service account; broader coverage is inferred from the active UID-range, systemd-default, and coredump policies.","The connection-reset results from cmd-5994a310b9904e71 and cmd-b3bfdddc56864c3f do not establish whether transient units were briefly created. The subsequent reboot and clean final unit state show no surviving failure or residue."]}
